Define a sequence  $a_{m,n}$  where  $a_{m,0}=1,$  and for all other  $m,n$  (assuming  $m \ge 1$ ):   $$ a_{m,n}=\begin{cases}
0 & n<0 
1 & n \equiv 0 \mod{m} 
a_{m,n-1}+a_{m, n-m} & \text{else}
\end{cases} $$   If  $\tfrac{a_{2025, (2025^2-1)}}{a_{2025, (2024^2-1)}} = \tfrac{a}{b}$  where  $a$  and  $b$  are relatively prime positive integers, then what is  $a+b$ ?

solution_md:
Let  $n\in\mathbb{Z}$  and  $p$  be prime.  We will be using Legendre's Formula, which states that  $$ v_p(n!)=\sum_{i\ge 1}\left\lfloor\frac{n}{p^i}\right\rfloor=\left\lfloor\frac{n}{p}\right\rfloor+\left\lfloor\frac{n}{p^2}\right\rfloor+\left\lfloor\frac{n}{p^3}\right\rfloor+\dots $$  and since  $12=2^2\cdot3=4\cdot3$  it suffices to look at  $v_2(66!)$  and  $v_3(66!)$ .  We see that
\begin{align*}
v_2(66!)&=\sum_{i\ge 1}\left\lfloor\frac{66}{p^i}\right\rfloor=\left\lfloor\frac{66}{2}\right\rfloor+\left\lfloor\frac{66}{4}\right\rfloor+\left\lfloor\frac{66}{8}\right\rfloor+\left\lfloor\frac{66}{16}\right\rfloor+\left\lfloor\frac{66}{32}\right\rfloor+\left\lfloor\frac{66}{64}\right\rfloor=64
v_3(66!)&=\sum_{i\ge 1}\left\lfloor\frac{66}{p^i}\right\rfloor=\left\lfloor\frac{66}{3}\right\rfloor+\left\lfloor\frac{66}{9}\right\rfloor+\left\lfloor\frac{66}{27}\right\rfloor=31.
\end{align*}
Hence, we see that  $2^{64}~|~66!\implies 4^{32}~|~66!$  and  $3^{31}~|~66!$ , so the largest  $k$  such that  $12^k~|~66!$  is  $k=\boxed{31}$ .

