We need  $2016^{239}\equiv 1\pmod{p}$  for prime  $p$  which means  $\text{ord}_p(2016)~|~239,1$ .  From here we have two cases.**Case 1**: Suppose that  $\text{ord}_p(2016)~|~1$ .  This means that we are looking for  $p$  such that  $2016\equiv 1\pmod p$ .  Clearly we see that  $$ n\equiv 1\pmod{p}\implies n-1\equiv 0\pmod{p}\implies p~|~n-1 $$  for any natural  $n$ , so we must have  $p~|~2015$ .  We have  $2015=5\cdot 13\cdot 31$  so our solutions are  $p=5,15,31$  for this case.**Case 2**: Suppose that  $\text{ord}_p(2016)~|~239$ .  We know that  $\text{ord}_p(a)~|~p-1$  and since  $\text{ord}_p(2016)~|~239$  we need  $239~|~p-1$  or  $p-1~|~239$ .  The latter is impossible since  $239$  is prime, so we attempt to find sufficient  $p$  such that  $239~|~p-1$ .   The smallest such  $p=479$ , and we can confirm that this is a solution since
\begin{align*}
2016^{478}\equiv 1\pmod{479}\implies 2016^{239}\equiv 1\pmod{479}
\end{align*}
by Fermat's Little Theorem.  This means the smallest solution is  $p=479$  for this case.

